<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Don (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tanais, River Don (Russia)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Don is a river of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 1,870 kilometres long, flowing from south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a>. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as the Tanais, it was regarded in antiquity as the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and the Sarmatians. Later it was the heartland of the Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>–Don Canal now links it to the Volga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/don-river.jpg" alt="Don (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: High Contrast · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_(Voronezh_Oblast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
